<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Pulled into directional-derivatives.ptx by XInclude.
     Requires in assets/ :  isotherm-runner.html, isotherm-runner-preview.png -->

<section xml:id="subsec-scalar-fields-potentials">
  <title>Scalar Fields, Potentials, and the Direction Nature Chooses</title>

  <p>
    A <term>scalar field</term> is a rule that assigns a single number to every point of
    a region: the temperature <m>T(x,y)</m> in a city, the pressure <m>P(x,y)</m> over an
    ocean, the elevation <m>h(x,y)</m> on a hillside, the concentration <m>c(x,y)</m> of a
    pollutant in a corridor. A scalar field carries no direction of its own. It is only a
    number at each location.
  </p>
  
  <p>
  Physics, by contrast, is full of quantities that point somewhere: heat flows,
   forces push, wind blows. These are <term>vector fields</term>. The gradient is
   what ties them back to scalar fields <mdash/> at least for the
   <term>conservative</term> fields taken up in MATH 14 at SCU, where the whole
   vector field is recovered from a single scalar potential by differentiation.
   Because such fields are so common in nature, the pattern in the table below
   appears again and again across physics.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a scalar field governs a physical force or flux, we call the field a
    <term>potential</term>, and the associated vector quantity is, up to a constant, the
    <em>negative</em> gradient of that potential.
  </p>

  <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
      <cell>Setting</cell>
      <cell>Scalar field</cell>
      <cell>Vector quantity</cell>
      <cell>Level curves are</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Heat conduction</cell>
      <cell>temperature <m>T</m></cell>
      <cell>heat flux <m>\mathbf q = -k\nabla T</m> (Fourier's law)</cell>
      <cell>isotherms</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Electrostatics</cell>
      <cell>potential <m>V</m></cell>
      <cell>electric field <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m></cell>
      <cell>equipotentials</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Mechanics</cell>
      <cell>potential energy <m>U</m></cell>
      <cell>conservative force <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m></cell>
      <cell>contours of constant energy</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Meteorology</cell>
      <cell>pressure <m>P</m></cell>
      <cell>pressure-gradient force <m>-\nabla P</m> per unit volume</cell>
      <cell>isobars</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>
    The minus sign in each of these laws encodes one physical fact:
    <em>nature moves downhill</em>. Heat drains from hot to cold, charges move toward low
    electric potential, a ball rolls toward low gravitational potential energy, air accelerates 
    toward low pressure. So the direction the physics actually selects is the direction of most rapid
    <em>decrease</em>, which is exactly the case <m>\theta = \pi</m> of
    <m>D_{\mathbf u}f = |\nabla f|\cos\theta</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three consequences carry over unchanged from
    <xref ref="sec-dirderiv-properties"/>, and they are worth restating in physical
    language.
  </p>

  <p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = 0</m>: fastest increase, at the rate <m>|\nabla f|</m>. This is the
        direction heat comes <em>from</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi</m>: fastest decrease, at the rate <m>-|\nabla f|</m>. This is the
        direction the flux, the force, or the wind actually points.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>: no change at all. These directions trace the level curves of
        the field <mdash/> isotherms, equipotentials, isobars, the contour lines on a
        topographic map. Since <m>\nabla f</m> is orthogonal to the level curve through a
        point, <em>the flux, the force, or the wind always crosses the
        contours at right angles</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  </p>

<section xml:id="sec-Examples-from-Mechanics">
  <title>Examples from Mechanics</title>
  
<p>
  You may recall in your introductory physics course that the gravitational potential energy <m>U</m> of a mass <m>m</m> at height <m>y</m> is
  <m>U = mgy</m>.  When the height remains constant, the potential energy is constant.  The surface of constant potential energy is
  level surface - or an equipotential surface.

  Using the potential, the gravitational force is
  <md>
    <mrow> {\mathbf F} \amp = -\nabla U </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -\frac{d}{dy}(mgy) \hat{\mathbf j}</mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -mg{\hat{\mathbf j}} </mrow>
  </md>
  which points straight down.  Note that the force is perpendicular to the level surface of constant potential energy.
</p>

<p>
  <xref ref="fig-gravity-potential-gradient"/> shows the two halves of this statement side by
  side. On the left is the physical picture: the mass, its height above the ground, and the
  horizontal surfaces on which <m>U = mgy</m> does not change. On the right the same surfaces
  are drawn again, now carrying the gradient and the force.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-gravity-potential-gradient">
  <caption>
    Gravitational potential energy near the earth's surface.
    <em>Left:</em> a mass <m>m</m> at heights <m>y_1 \lt y_2 \lt y_3</m>. Since <m>U = mgy</m>
    depends on the height alone, each dashed horizontal line is an equipotential surface: every
    point on it carries the same potential energy.
    <em>Right:</em> at every point <m>\nabla U = mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m> points straight up,
    the direction in which <m>U</m> increases fastest, and the force
    <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U = -mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m> points straight down. The field is
    uniform, so the arrows are the same at every point, and both are perpendicular to the
    equipotential surfaces, as the right angles record.
  </caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
    <figure xml:id="fig-gravity-height">
      <caption>The mass and its height above the surface of the earth.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-gravity-height" width="100%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-gravity-height">
        <diagram dimensions="(400,333)" margins="6">
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.7,-1.15,7.4,5.6)">

            <!-- the surface of the earth -->
            <group at="ga-ground">
              <rectangle lower-left="(-0.4,-0.45)" dimensions="(6.7,0.45)" fill="#EFEFEF" stroke="#EFEFEF"/>
              <line p1="(-0.4,0)" p2="(6.3,0)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
              <repeat parameter="k=0..18">
                <line p1="(-0.1+0.35*k,0)" p2="(-0.4+0.35*k,-0.32)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1"/>
              </repeat>
            </group>
            <label anchor="(2.9,-0.5)" alignment="s" color="#4D4D4D" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{surface of the earth}</m>
            </label>

            <!-- height axis -->
            <line at="ga-axis" p1="(0.5,-0.05)" p2="(0.5,4.55)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5,4.55)" alignment="ne"><m>y</m></label>

            <!-- equipotential surfaces -->
            <line at="ga-level1" p1="(0.15,1.2)" p2="(4.6,1.2)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="ga-level2" p1="(0.15,2.4)" p2="(4.6,2.4)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="ga-level3" p1="(0.15,3.6)" p2="(4.6,3.6)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <label anchor="(4.7,1.2)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(4.7,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(4.7,3.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_3</m></label>

            <!-- ticks -->
            <group at="ga-ticks">
              <line p1="(0.38,1.2)" p2="(0.62,1.2)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="1.5"/>
              <line p1="(0.38,2.4)" p2="(0.62,2.4)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="1.5"/>
              <line p1="(0.38,3.6)" p2="(0.62,3.6)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="1.5"/>
            </group>
            <label anchor="(0.3,1.2)" alignment="w"><m>y_1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.3,2.4)" alignment="w"><m>y_2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.3,3.6)" alignment="w"><m>y_3</m></label>

            <!-- the object and its height -->
            <line at="ga-height" p1="(2.9,0.03)" p2="(2.9,2.2)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
            <label anchor="(2.98,1.7)" alignment="e"><m>y_2</m></label>
            <point at="ga-mass" p="(2.9,2.4)" size="7" fill="#D55E00"/>
            <label anchor="(2.9,2.4)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,4)"><m>m</m></label>

            <!-- annotations on the picture -->
            <label anchor="(2.45,4.0)" alignment="n" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{same height} \Rightarrow \text{same } U</m>
            </label>
            <line p1="(3.2,3.98)" p2="(3.55,3.66)" stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(3.3,0.72)" alignment="e" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{height above the surface}</m>
            </label>
            <line p1="(3.22,0.75)" p2="(2.96,0.92)" stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(4.95,5.15)" alignment="e"><m>U = mgy</m></label>

          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A mass at height y above the surface of the earth, with three dashed horizontal lines of constant gravitational potential energy.">
              <annotation ref="ga-ground" text="The surface of the earth, a hatched horizontal line across the bottom of the figure."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-axis" text="A vertical axis labelled y, measuring height above the surface."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-level1" text="The equipotential surface at height y one, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y one. It is the lowest of the three, so it carries the least potential energy."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-level2" text="The equipotential surface at height y two, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y two. The mass rests on this line."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-level3" text="The equipotential surface at height y three, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y three, the highest of the three and the one carrying the most potential energy."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-mass" text="The mass m, drawn as a small filled circle sitting on the middle equipotential surface."/>
              <annotation ref="ga-height" text="A double headed arrow from the ground up to the mass, marking its height y two above the surface."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-gravity-force-gradient">
      <caption>
        The gradient, the force, and the equipotential surfaces they cross at right angles.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-gravity-force-gradient" width="100%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-gravity-force-gradient">
        <diagram dimensions="(400,333)" margins="6">
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.7,-1.15,7.4,5.6)">

            <!-- the surface of the earth -->
            <group at="gb-ground">
              <rectangle lower-left="(-0.4,-0.45)" dimensions="(6.7,0.45)" fill="#EFEFEF" stroke="#EFEFEF"/>
              <line p1="(-0.4,0)" p2="(6.3,0)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
              <repeat parameter="k=0..18">
                <line p1="(-0.1+0.35*k,0)" p2="(-0.4+0.35*k,-0.32)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1"/>
              </repeat>
            </group>
            <label anchor="(2.9,-0.5)" alignment="s" color="#4D4D4D" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{surface of the earth}</m>
            </label>

            <!-- height axis -->
            <line at="gb-axis" p1="(0.5,-0.05)" p2="(0.5,4.55)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5,4.55)" alignment="ne"><m>y</m></label>

            <!-- the same equipotential surfaces -->
            <line at="gb-level1" p1="(0.15,1.2)" p2="(4.6,1.2)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="gb-level2" p1="(0.15,2.4)" p2="(4.6,2.4)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="gb-level3" p1="(0.15,3.6)" p2="(4.6,3.6)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <label anchor="(4.7,1.2)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(4.7,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(4.7,3.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = mgy_3</m></label>

            <!-- gradient and force on the lowest surface -->
            <vector at="gb-grad1" v="(0,0.65)" tail="(1.4,1.2)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="gb-force1" v="(0,-0.65)" tail="(1.4,1.2)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="gb-right-angle1">
              <line p1="(1.4,0.98)" p2="(1.62,0.98)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(1.62,0.98)" p2="(1.62,1.2)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>

            <!-- gradient and force where the mass sits -->
            <point at="gb-mass" p="(2.9,2.4)" size="6" fill="#BFBFBF"/>
            <vector at="gb-grad2" v="(0,0.65)" tail="(2.9,2.4)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="gb-force2" v="(0,-0.65)" tail="(2.9,2.4)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="gb-right-angle2">
              <line p1="(2.9,2.18)" p2="(3.12,2.18)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(3.12,2.18)" p2="(3.12,2.4)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>
            <label anchor="(2.98,3.05)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\nabla U</m></label>
            <label anchor="(2.98,1.75)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m></label>

            <!-- gradient and force on the highest surface -->
            <vector at="gb-grad3" v="(0,0.65)" tail="(4.1,3.6)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="gb-force3" v="(0,-0.65)" tail="(4.1,3.6)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="gb-right-angle3">
              <line p1="(4.1,3.38)" p2="(4.32,3.38)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(4.32,3.38)" p2="(4.32,3.6)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>

            <!-- annotations on the picture -->
            <label anchor="(0.6,4.15)" alignment="e" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{the same arrows everywhere}</m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(2.2,0.68)" alignment="e" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{meets every level surface at } 90^\circ</m>
            </label>
            <line p1="(2.12,0.72)" p2="(1.74,0.95)" stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(4.95,5.3)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\nabla U = mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m></label>
            <label anchor="(4.95,4.6)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>\mathbf F = -mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m></label>

          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="Upward gradient arrows and downward force arrows meeting the horizontal equipotential surfaces at right angles.">
              <annotation ref="gb-ground" text="The surface of the earth, a hatched horizontal line across the bottom of the figure."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-axis" text="A vertical axis labelled y, measuring height above the surface."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-level1" text="The lowest equipotential surface, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y one."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-level2" text="The middle equipotential surface, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y two, the one the mass sits on."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-level3" text="The highest equipotential surface, a dashed blue horizontal line labelled U equals m g y three."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-grad2" text="The gradient of U at the mass, a green arrow of length m g pointing straight up, in the direction in which the potential energy increases fastest."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-force2" text="The gravitational force at the mass, a vermillion arrow of the same length pointing straight down, opposite the gradient, in the direction in which the potential energy decreases fastest."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-right-angle2" text="A small square marking the right angle between these arrows and the equipotential surface through the mass."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-grad1" text="The gradient at a second point, on the lowest surface: an identical green arrow pointing straight up, since the field is uniform."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-force1" text="The force at that second point, an identical vermillion arrow pointing straight down."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-grad3" text="The gradient at a third point, on the highest surface: again the same green arrow pointing straight up."/>
              <annotation ref="gb-force3" text="The force at that third point, again the same vermillion arrow pointing straight down."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>

<p>
  <xref ref="fig-spring-potential-gradient"/> tells the same story for the spring. Here the
  potential energy depends only on <m>x</m>, so its level curves in the plane are the vertical
  lines <m>x = \text{constant}</m>, and the force, which is horizontal, meets every one of them
  at a right angle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-spring-potential-gradient">
  <caption>
    A block on a spring.
    <em>Left:</em> the block displaced a distance <m>x</m> from equilibrium, drawn above the
    graph of its potential energy <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m>; the two pictures share the same
    horizontal scale, so the block sits directly above its own point on the parabola.
    <em>Right:</em> because <m>U</m> depends on <m>x</m> alone, the equipotential curves are the
    vertical lines <m>x = \text{constant}</m>. The gradient
    <m>\nabla U = kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m> points away from equilibrium, the force
    <m>\mathbf F = -kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m> points back toward it, and both cross the
    equipotential lines at right angles. The arrows are twice as long on the outer lines
    because <m>|\nabla U| = k|x|</m>.
  </caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
    <figure xml:id="fig-spring-displacement">
      <caption>
        The block, its displacement from equilibrium, and the potential energy
        <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spring-displacement" width="100%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spring-displacement">
        <diagram dimensions="(400,285)" margins="6">
          <definition>U(x) = -2.2 + 0.6*(x-3)^2</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.75,-3.05,6.55,2.15)">

            <!-- wall -->
            <group at="sa-wall">
              <rectangle lower-left="(-0.45,0)" dimensions="(0.45,1.7)" fill="#EFEFEF" stroke="#EFEFEF"/>
              <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(0,1.7)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
              <repeat parameter="k=0..4">
                <line p1="(0,0.35+0.3*k)" p2="(-0.35,0.07+0.3*k)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1"/>
              </repeat>
            </group>

            <!-- floor -->
            <group at="sa-floor">
              <line p1="(-0.45,0)" p2="(5.3,0)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
              <repeat parameter="k=0..14">
                <line p1="(-0.15+0.4*k,0)" p2="(-0.45+0.4*k,-0.3)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1"/>
              </repeat>
            </group>

            <!-- the spring -->
            <definition>coil(t) = (t, 0.5 + 0.19*sin(19*t))</definition>
            <parametric-curve at="sa-spring" function="coil" domain="(0,3.65)" N="500"
                              stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>

            <!-- the block, displaced to the right of equilibrium -->
            <rectangle at="sa-block" lower-left="(3.65,0)" dimensions="(0.7,0.95)"
                       fill="#D55E00" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(4.55,0.48)" alignment="e"><m>m</m></label>

            <!-- equilibrium and displaced positions, shared by both pictures -->
            <line at="sa-equilibrium" p1="(3,1.5)" p2="(3,-2.35)" stroke="#808080" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line p1="(4,1.2)" p2="(4,-1.6)" stroke="#808080" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <label anchor="(3,1.55)" alignment="n" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{equilibrium } (x=0)</m>
            </label>

            <!-- the displacement -->
            <line at="sa-displacement" p1="(3,1.2)" p2="(4,1.2)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
            <label anchor="(3.5,1.22)" alignment="n"><m>x</m></label>

            <!-- the potential energy graph, on the same horizontal scale -->
            <line at="sa-xaxis" p1="(1.2,-2.2)" p2="(5.1,-2.2)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(5.1,-2.2)" alignment="ne"><m>x</m></label>
            <line at="sa-Uaxis" p1="(3,-2.35)" p2="(3,-0.7)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(2.94,-0.72)" alignment="w"><m>U</m></label>
            <graph at="sa-parabola" function="U" domain="(1.4,4.6)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
            <label anchor="(4.72,-0.95)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m></label>

            <!-- the block's own point on the parabola -->
            <point at="sa-energy" p="(4,-1.6)" size="5" fill="#D55E00"/>
            <line p1="(3,-1.6)" p2="(4,-1.6)" stroke="#808080" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <label anchor="(2.94,-1.6)" alignment="w"><m>\frac{1}{2}kx^2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(3.12,-2.62)" alignment="e" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\nabla U = 0 \text{ at equilibrium}</m>
            </label>

            <!-- annotation on the picture -->
            <label anchor="(1.45,1.05)" alignment="n" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{spring stretched}</m>
            </label>
            <line p1="(1.7,1.02)" p2="(1.95,0.74)" stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1"/>

          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A block on a horizontal spring displaced from equilibrium, drawn above the parabola giving its potential energy.">
              <annotation ref="sa-wall" text="A hatched wall at the left, holding the fixed end of the spring."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-floor" text="A hatched horizontal floor the block slides along."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-spring" text="The spring, drawn as a blue coil running from the wall to the block."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-block" text="The block of mass m, pulled to the right of its equilibrium position so that the spring is stretched."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-equilibrium" text="A dashed vertical line marking the equilibrium position, where x equals zero. It runs the full height of the figure, linking the block picture above to the energy graph below."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-displacement" text="A double headed arrow between the equilibrium line and the block, marking the displacement x."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-xaxis" text="The horizontal axis of the energy graph, labelled x, on the same scale as the picture above it."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-Uaxis" text="The vertical axis of the energy graph, labelled U, rising from the equilibrium position."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-parabola" text="The graph of the potential energy U equals one half k x squared, an upward opening parabola with its vertex at the equilibrium position. It is symmetric, so stretching and compressing by the same amount store the same energy."/>
              <annotation ref="sa-energy" text="The point on the parabola directly below the block, giving the energy one half k x squared stored in the spring at that displacement."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-spring-force-gradient">
      <caption>
        The gradient and the force, orthogonal to the equipotential lines
        <m>x = \text{constant}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spring-force-gradient" width="100%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spring-force-gradient">
        <diagram dimensions="(400,292)" margins="6">
          <coordinates bbox="(-4.3,-3.35,4.9,3.35)">

            <!-- axes, with the equilibrium position at the origin -->
            <line at="sb-xaxis" p1="(-3.5,0)" p2="(3.6,0)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(3.6,0)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
            <line at="sb-yaxis" p1="(0,-2)" p2="(0,2.3)" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0,2.3)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>

            <!-- equipotential curves: U is constant on each vertical line -->
            <line at="sb-level-a" p1="(1.1,-1.75)" p2="(1.1,1.8)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="sb-level-ma" p1="(-1.1,-1.75)" p2="(-1.1,1.8)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="sb-level-2a" p1="(2.2,-1.75)" p2="(2.2,1.8)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <line at="sb-level-m2a" p1="(-2.2,-1.75)" p2="(-2.2,1.8)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>
            <label anchor="(-1.1,1.85)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2"><m>U = \frac{1}{2}ka^2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(2.2,1.85)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2"><m>U = 2ka^2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(1.1,-1.8)" alignment="s"><m>a</m></label>
            <label anchor="(2.2,-1.8)" alignment="s"><m>2a</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-1.1,-1.8)" alignment="s"><m>-a</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-2.2,-1.8)" alignment="s"><m>-2a</m></label>

            <!-- at x = a : gradient outward, force inward -->
            <vector at="sb-grad-a" v="(0.5,0)" tail="(1.1,0.8)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="sb-force-a" v="(-0.5,0)" tail="(1.1,0.8)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="sb-right-angle-a">
              <line p1="(1.28,0.8)" p2="(1.28,0.62)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(1.28,0.62)" p2="(1.1,0.62)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>

            <!-- at x = 2a : twice as long, since the gradient has size k times the absolute value of x -->
            <vector at="sb-grad-2a" v="(1,0)" tail="(2.2,-0.8)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="sb-force-2a" v="(-1,0)" tail="(2.2,-0.8)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="sb-right-angle-2a">
              <line p1="(2.38,-0.8)" p2="(2.38,-0.98)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(2.38,-0.98)" p2="(2.2,-0.98)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>
            <label anchor="(3.25,-0.8)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\nabla U</m></label>

            <!-- at x = -a -->
            <vector at="sb-grad-ma" v="(-0.5,0)" tail="(-1.1,-0.8)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="sb-force-ma" v="(0.5,0)" tail="(-1.1,-0.8)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="sb-right-angle-ma">
              <line p1="(-1.28,-0.8)" p2="(-1.28,-0.98)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(-1.28,-0.98)" p2="(-1.1,-0.98)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>

            <!-- at x = -2a -->
            <vector at="sb-grad-m2a" v="(-1,0)" tail="(-2.2,0.8)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="sb-force-m2a" v="(1,0)" tail="(-2.2,0.8)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
            <group at="sb-right-angle-m2a">
              <line p1="(-2.38,0.8)" p2="(-2.38,0.62)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
              <line p1="(-2.38,0.62)" p2="(-2.2,0.62)" stroke="#666666" thickness="1"/>
            </group>

            <!-- annotations on the picture -->
            <label anchor="(0,-2.3)" alignment="s" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{equipotential curves: the vertical lines } x = \text{constant}</m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(0,-2.85)" alignment="s" color="#666666" scale="0.85">
              <m>\text{the arrows grow with } |x|, \text{ since } |\nabla U| = k|x|</m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-3.5,2.95)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\nabla U = kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m></label>
            <label anchor="(1.1,2.95)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>\mathbf F = -kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m></label>

          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="Vertical equipotential lines of the spring potential energy, with horizontal gradient arrows pointing away from equilibrium and force arrows pointing back toward it.">
              <annotation ref="sb-xaxis" text="The horizontal x axis, with the equilibrium position of the spring at the origin."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-yaxis" text="The vertical y axis."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-level-a" text="The equipotential line x equals a, a dashed blue vertical line to the right of the origin, on which U equals one half k a squared."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-level-ma" text="The equipotential line x equals minus a, the mirror image on the left, carrying the same energy one half k a squared."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-level-2a" text="The equipotential line x equals two a, twice as far from the origin, on which U equals two k a squared, four times as much energy."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-level-m2a" text="The equipotential line x equals minus two a, the mirror image on the left."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-grad-a" text="The gradient at a point on the line x equals a, a short green arrow pointing to the right, away from equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-force-a" text="The spring force at that point, a short vermillion arrow of equal length pointing to the left, back toward equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-right-angle-a" text="A small square marking the right angle where these horizontal arrows meet the vertical equipotential line."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-grad-2a" text="The gradient at a point on the line x equals two a, a green arrow pointing away from equilibrium and twice as long, since its size is k times the distance from equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-force-2a" text="The spring force there, an equally long vermillion arrow pointing back toward equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-grad-ma" text="The gradient on the line x equals minus a, a short green arrow pointing to the left, again away from equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-force-ma" text="The force there, a short vermillion arrow pointing to the right, back toward equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-grad-m2a" text="The gradient on the line x equals minus two a, a long green arrow pointing to the left, away from equilibrium."/>
              <annotation ref="sb-force-m2a" text="The force there, a long vermillion arrow pointing to the right, back toward equilibrium."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>

</section>


  <example xml:id="ex-two-runners">
    <title>Two Runners in a City Heat Island</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        On a still summer afternoon the pavement downtown is far hotter than the outlying
        neighborhoods. Model the surface air temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, by
        <md>T(x,y) = 74 + 14\,e^{-(x^2 + 3y^2)/40},</md>
        where <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are measured in miles east and north of downtown. The
        core reaches <m>88^\circ</m>F, and the outskirts settle near <m>74^\circ</m>F. Two
        runners meet at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The first runner is overheating and wants cooler air as quickly as possible.
              Which direction should she take, and how fast does the temperature fall along
              it?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The second runner has settled into the temperature at <m>P_0</m> and wants a
              route on which it never changes at all. What path should she run?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiating the exponential with the chain rule,
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla T \amp = -\frac{14}{40}\,e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}\left(2x\,\mathbf i + 6y\,\mathbf j\right),</mrow>
          <mrow>(\nabla T)_{(1,1)} \amp = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\left(2\,\mathbf i + 6\,\mathbf j\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -0.633\,\mathbf i - 1.900\,\mathbf j .</mrow>
        </md>
        At <m>P_0</m> the temperature itself is
        <m>T(1,1) = 74 + 14e^{-1/10} \approx 86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 1.</term> Temperature falls fastest in the direction of
        <m>-\nabla T</m>, which is a positive multiple of <m>\mathbf i + 3\mathbf j</m>.
        Normalizing, the heading is north-northeast:
        <md>\mathbf u = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf i + \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf j,</md>
        and the rate of change along it is
        <md>
          <mrow>D_{\mathbf u} T \amp = -|\nabla T|
                = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\sqrt{4 + 36}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -2.0 \ \frac{{}^\circ\text{F}}{\text{mi}} .</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice how modest this is. The gradient hands her the <em>best available</em>
        direction, not a dramatic one: even running the optimal heading, she must cover a
        full mile to shed about two degrees. Any other heading does worse.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 2.</term> The second runner needs <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>, that is,
        <m>\mathbf u</m> orthogonal to <m>\nabla T</m>:
        <md>\mathbf u = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\left(3\,\mathbf i - \mathbf j\right).</md>
        Following that condition continuously, rather than for a single step, means never
        leaving the level curve of <m>T</m> through <m>P_0</m>. Since <m>T</m> is constant
        exactly when <m>x^2 + 3y^2</m> is constant, her route is
        <md>x^2 + 3y^2 = 4,</md>
        an ellipse with semi-axes <m>2</m> miles east-west and
        <m>2/\sqrt{3} \approx 1.15</m> miles north-south <mdash/> a closed loop of roughly
        ten miles, every step of it at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>

    <p>
      In the animation below, switch between the two runners. On the loop the thermometer
      holds at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F while the blue gradient arrow stays stubbornly
      perpendicular to her heading; on the escape route the same reading falls steadily, and
      the path cuts across every isotherm at a right angle.
    </p>
      <p>
        Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
        switching between the two routes and watching how the readings respond as the
        runner moves.
      </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-isotherm-runner">
      <caption>
        Two routes from <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the field
        <m>T(x,y) = 74 + 14e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}</m>. The highlighted ellipse is the
        level curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, on which <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isotherm-runner"
                   iframe="isotherm-runner.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="6:5">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Press <q>Run the isotherm</q> to send the runner around the level
            curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, or <q>Dash for cool air</q> to send her
            along the path of steepest cooling. The readout shows her
            temperature, position, directional derivative
            <m>D_{\mathbf u}T</m>, and distance travelled; the blue arrow is
            <m>\nabla T</m> at her position. Use <q>Pace</q> to change her
            speed, and <q>Pause</q> or <q>Reset</q> to control the animation.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>


</section>
